hyperdeterminant -- computes the hyperdeterminant of a boundary format tensor
*****************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        hyperdeterminant f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note map of labeled modules: LabeledModuleMap,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, 

Description
===========

This constructs the hyperdeterminant of a tensor of boundary format, where we
say that a $a\times b_1\times \dots \times b_n$ has boundary format if $$
a-\sum_{i=1}^n (b_i-1)=1. $$ We construct the hyperdeterminant as the
determinant of a certain square matrix derived from $f$.  The hyperdeterminant
function outputs the hyperdeterminant itself, whereas the *note
hyperdeterminantMatrix: hyperdeterminantMatrix, function outputs the matrix
used to compute the hyperdeterminant.  (For background on computing
hyperdeterminants, see Section 14.3 of the book ``Discriminants, resultants,
and multidimensional determinants '' by Gelfand-Kapranov-Zelevinsky.)

The following constructs the generic hyperdeterminant of format $3\times
2\times 2$, which is a polynomial of degree 6 consisting of 66 monomials.



+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : f=flattenedGenericTensor({3,2,2},QQ);                  |
|                                                            |
|                                4                          3|
|o1 : Matrix (QQ[x     ..x     ])  <--- (QQ[x     ..x     ]) |
|                 0,0,0   2,1,1              0,0,0   2,1,1   |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S=ring f;                                              |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : h=hyperdeterminant f;                                  |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : degree h                                               |
|                                                            |
|o4 = {6}                                                    |
|                                                            |
|o4 : List                                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : #terms h                                               |
|                                                            |
|o5 = 66                                                     |
+------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

There is bug involving the graded structure of the output. Namely, the code
assumes that all entries of f have degree 1, and gives the wrong graded
structure if this is not the case. If ring f is not graded, then the code gives
an error.



See also
========

  * *note hyperdeterminantMatrix: hyperdeterminantMatrix, -- computes a matrix
    whose determinant equals the hyperdeterminant of a boundary format tensor

Ways to use hyperdeterminant:
=============================

  * "hyperdeterminant(LabeledModuleMap)"

For the programmer
==================

