P^{b_n-1})$, and let $$ \mathbf K := \cdots \wedge^2 \oplus_1^a O_X(-1,\dots,
-1) \to  O_X  \to 0 $$ be the Koszul complex of the multilinear forms
corresponding to f, on $X$. The output of tensorComplex1(f,w) is the first map
of the complex obtained by pushing $\mathbf K \otimes {\mathcal
O}_X(w_1,\dots,w_n)$ down to $Spec S$.

This script implements the construction of tensor complexes from the paper
``Tensor Complexes: Multilinear free resolutions constructed from higher
tensors'' of Berkesch, Erman, Kummini and Sam (BEKS).

The program requires that $f$ is a flattened tensor, that is, a map $A \to
B_1\otimes\cdots\otimes B_n$. Returns the first map in the tensor complex
$F(f,w)$ of BEKS, requiring that $w$ satisfies: $$ w_0 = 0, w_1 \geq 0, w_2
\geq w_1+b_1, \ {\rm and }\  w_i>w_{i-1} \ {\rm for }\ i\geq 2. $$

When $rank A=\sum rank B_i$, that is, $L_0 = \sum_{i=1}^n L_i$ then we are in
the ``balanced case'' discussed in Section 3 of BEKS. In this case giving a
weight vector is unnecessary, and one can use the format tensorComplex1 f.

The example from section 12 of BEKS appears below.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : f = flattenedGenericTensor({4,2,2},ZZ/32003)                            |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = | x_(0,0,0) x_(1,0,0) x_(2,0,0) x_(3,0,0) |                             |
|     | x_(0,0,1) x_(1,0,1) x_(2,0,1) x_(3,0,1) |                             |
|     | x_(0,1,0) x_(1,1,0) x_(2,1,0) x_(3,1,0) |                             |
|     | x_(0,1,1) x_(1,1,1) x_(2,1,1) x_(3,1,1) |                             |
|                                                                             |
|               ZZ                  4         ZZ                  4           |
|o1 : Matrix (-----[x     ..x     ])  <--- (-----[x     ..x     ])            |
|             32003  0,0,0   3,1,1          32003  0,0,0   3,1,1              |
